  <standard_solution_analysis>
    <approach>Strong induction on n. Let p be the largest prime divisor of n. In any factorization n = ∏ x_j, one factor x_i is divisible by p, say x_i = p·d₁ where d₁ | n/p. This gives f(n) ≤ Σ_{d₁ | n/p} f(n/(pd₁)).</approach>
    <key_steps>
      <step>1. Base case: trivial for small n.</step>
      <step>2. Establish injection: each factorization of n maps to (d₁, factorization of n/(pd₁)), giving f(n) ≤ Σ_{d₁|n/p} f(n/(pd₁)).</step>
      <step>3. Apply inductive hypothesis: f(k) ≤ k/Q(k) where Q(k) is the largest prime factor of k.</step>
      <step>4. Bound k/Q(k) ≤ φ(k) (Euler's totient), since Q(k)·∏(1-1/p) = (Q(k)-1)·∏_{p≠Q}(1-1/p) ≥ 1 for all k>1.</step>
      <step>5. Apply totient identity: Σ_{d|m} φ(m/d) = m, yielding f(n) ≤ Σ φ(n/(pd₁)) = n/p.</step>
    </key_steps>
    <correctness>VALID</correctness>
    <correctness_notes>
      The injection argument is sound: in any factorization of n, at least one factor must be divisible by p (since p|n). Selecting that factor as x_i = p·d₁ and mapping to (d₁, remaining factorization) is injective. The bound k/Q(k) ≤ φ(k) holds because (Q(k)-1)·∏_{p|k, p≠Q(k)}(1-1/p) ≥ 1 for all k>1 (worst case k=2·Q gives (Q-1)/2 ≥ 1 for Q≥3; k=2 gives 1). The totient identity Σ_{d|m} φ(d) = m is classical and correctly applied.
    </correctness_notes>
    <answer_format>\boxed{\frac{n}{p}}</answer_format>
